---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import CompressImageToTarget from '@/components/tools/image-edit/CompressImageToTarget.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="compress-image-to-1mb">
  <CompressImageToTarget client:load targetKb={1024} inputLabel="Drop the large photos that need to fit 1 MB" />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>A megabyte is enough for almost anything</h2>
    <p>
      At 1 MB you can usually keep all pixels of a 24-megapixel photograph at a quality setting in the
      seventies or eighties. Use this target when the file must stay large: a CMS upload limit, a print
      preview, a photo competition entry, a portfolio submission. For a web page, 200 KB is the better
      target.
    </p>

    <h2>Bytes are not the same question as print quality</h2>
    <p>
      A common mistake: someone is told that a print needs “at least 1 MB” and thinks a bigger file
      gives a better print. A printer needs pixels. The guide is 240–300 pixels per printed inch:
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Print size</th><th>Pixels needed at 300 DPI</th><th>Megapixels</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>6 × 4 in</td><td>1800 × 1200</td><td>2.2 MP</td></tr>
          <tr><td>A5</td><td>1748 × 2480</td><td>4.3 MP</td></tr>
          <tr><td>A4</td><td>2480 × 3508</td><td>8.7 MP</td></tr>
          <tr><td>A3</td><td>3508 × 4961</td><td>17.4 MP</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Poster, viewed at a distance</td><td>150 DPI is fine</td><td>Quarter of the above</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Compression to 1 MB does not change these numbers, because the pixel count stays the same unless
      the quality search fails. Compare the dimensions in the result card with the table, then set the
      DPI tag with <a href="/change-image-dpi">Change Image DPI</a> so your printer uses the intended
      physical size.
    </p>

    <h2>When the search resizes anyway</h2>
    <p>
      Very large or very detailed images (45-megapixel raw exports, panoramas, dense foliage) can be
      over 1 MB even at low quality. The tool then starts its second pass, searches the pixel scale, and
      reports the percentage it used. If resolution matters more than the byte target, turn the
      fallback off. You then get the smallest full-resolution file and a note that the target was not
      met.
    </p>

    <h2>Reading the working</h2>
    <p>
      Expand “Show the search” to see each encode: quality, scale and size, marked as fitting or too
      large. For a 1 MB target on a typical photo, the first attempt at quality 92 usually fits. The
      tool then stops and gives you that highest-quality file, with no further compression.
    </p>

    <h2>Format notes at this size</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>JPEG is the correct default for photographs that go anywhere other than your own website.</li>
      <li>WebP saves about a third of the bytes, so at this size you keep a noticeably higher quality setting. All modern browsers read it; some desktop print software does not.</li>
      <li>PNG is useful for screenshots, charts and scans of line art. These compress losslessly to well under a megabyte, and JPEG would add ringing around each edge.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>MB, MiB and the safety margin</h2>
    <p>
      The tool counts 1 MB as 1024 KB (1,048,576 bytes) and aims 2% under it. The output thus satisfies
      both the 1024-based definition of your operating system and the 1,000,000-byte definition of some
      upload validators. The result always shows the exact byte count.
    </p>

    <h2>Other targets</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="/compress-image-to-500kb">500 KB</a> ·
      <a href="/compress-image-to-200kb">200 KB</a> ·
      <a href="/compress-image-to-100kb">100 KB</a> ·
      <a href="/reduce-image-size-in-kb">any target you type</a>.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
